WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.389
We have a whole bunch of economic news, we
have GDP numbers.

00:00:03.389 --> 00:00:06.000
We also have new unemployment numbers.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.740
Unemployment numbers probably will not talk
about today, probably tomorrow.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:11.349
But I do want to talk about GDP.

00:00:11.349 --> 00:00:13.849
So we are in the middle of a pandemic.

00:00:13.849 --> 00:00:20.220
We anticipated that Q1 GDP numbers, quote,
Q1, of course, January, February, March would

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.400
not be great.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.279
Q2, in which we are right now, we expect to
be really bad.

00:00:25.279 --> 00:00:30.510
And then there's certainly a question mark
around Q3 and the idea that Q4 numbers will

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:32.369
be dramatically recovered.

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:35.510
So what does the first quarter look like?

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:37.140
Why do we care about GDP?

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:43.450
Well, when we're trying to figure out the
economic impact of the Corona virus, we look

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:47.550
at the stock market, even though it doesn't
really tell us that much.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:51.700
And we've talked about why before we look
at unemployment, how many people are working,

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.170
how many people are losing their jobs.

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:57.460
We look at wages and we've also been waiting
on GDP.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:03.020
Q1 GDP was down 4.8 percent.

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:10.049
This is the largest drop since late 2008 in
the throes of the Great Recession.

00:01:10.049 --> 00:01:14.499
This is the first GDP decline at all in six
years.

00:01:14.499 --> 00:01:18.780
And it's not surprising we were expecting
this to understand these numbers.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.799
First, it helps to know what GDP is.

00:01:20.799 --> 00:01:24.039
GDP is the gross domestic product.

00:01:24.039 --> 00:01:28.829
That's the total value of goods produced and
services provided off you.

00:01:28.829 --> 00:01:35.049
If you have two businesses, a factory and
a hair salon, for lack of a better term, for

00:01:35.049 --> 00:01:40.109
lack of a better example, the factory produces
one hundred dollars worth of goods.

00:01:40.109 --> 00:01:44.029
The hair salon provides one hundred dollars
worth of haircuts.

00:01:44.029 --> 00:01:49.469
That's two hundred dollars in total product
as this GDP is measured.

00:01:49.469 --> 00:01:53.060
Of course, there are many other elements that
go into it.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:58.070
It went down in the first quarter because
we had January and February, which were reasonably

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:00.950
normal months as far as the economy's concerned.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:05.469
And then in March, stay at home orders started
because of Corona virus.

00:02:05.469 --> 00:02:10.810
Businesses shut down, fewer things were sold,
fewer people were working, fewer services

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:16.409
were provided in the second quarter, which
is April, May, June, we are about a third

00:02:16.409 --> 00:02:19.150
of the way through Q2.

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:22.989
There's the idea that GDP could be down as
much as 20 percent.

00:02:22.989 --> 00:02:26.069
Some people are even throwing around the number
30 percent, which is devastating.

00:02:26.069 --> 00:02:28.610
These are we've actually become numb.

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:32.440
Today we learned another 3.8, 3.6, something
like that.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:35.110
Million people filed for unemployment last
week.

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:37.519
That's way down from a few weeks ago.

00:02:37.519 --> 00:02:43.209
But it is an insane number, historically speaking,
and we've become numb to these numbers of

00:02:43.209 --> 00:02:46.010
absolute economic devastation.

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:49.959
So now let's go beyond the headlines and really
dig into some of the data that made up the

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:54.709
GDP decline of 4.8 percent, because that may
not be what you expected.

00:02:54.709 --> 00:02:55.709
It's very interesting.

00:02:55.709 --> 00:03:01.500
So first of all, we can separate personal
consumption into goods and services, as I

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.430
mentioned before.

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:06.819
Goods and services combine to make up GDP.

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:09.400
Goods were actually almost unchanged.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:13.909
Goods as part of Q1 GDP were only down a tiny
bit.

00:03:13.909 --> 00:03:15.969
Now, maybe this is intuitive.

00:03:15.969 --> 00:03:18.010
Maybe it's not durable.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:21.629
Goods like cars and furnishings were certainly
down.

00:03:21.629 --> 00:03:23.099
There's not a lot of car sales going on.

00:03:23.099 --> 00:03:25.349
Many dealerships are closed.

00:03:25.349 --> 00:03:26.349
Furnishings.

00:03:26.349 --> 00:03:29.769
People aren't really going out and buying
too much furniture right now.

00:03:29.769 --> 00:03:32.810
But nondurable goods were actually up about
1 percent.

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:37.849
So that includes, of course, food and beverages
that are consumed off premises, meaning grocery

00:03:37.849 --> 00:03:38.849
stores.

00:03:38.849 --> 00:03:39.890
That makes sense.

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:45.370
Goods almost unchanged overall in total services,
of course, were down.

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:50.069
Household spending on services was down pretty
significantly, about 6 percent.

00:03:50.069 --> 00:03:54.340
But there was also a major decrease in health
care spending.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:04:00.019
And this is something that really tells us
a lot about how our economy is organized and

00:04:00.019 --> 00:04:01.939
not particularly for the better.

00:04:01.939 --> 00:04:04.829
So you might imagine wait to think we've got
a pandemic.

00:04:04.829 --> 00:04:09.340
Everything that's going on right now is because
of a medical situation that people need treatment

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:10.340
for.

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:12.730
People are filling hospitals for.

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:17.830
How is it that with a million, almost 1.1
million confirmed cases of coronavirus in

00:04:17.830 --> 00:04:19.209
the United States.

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:21.940
Health care spending was down during Q1.

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:27.440
And the answer is that routine health care
is almost completely shut down.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:34.539
Everything from dermatology visits for a skin
rash to elective and non-emergency surgery.

00:04:34.539 --> 00:04:36.080
It's all but canceled.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:37.730
It's all but canceled.

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:42.830
This also reminds us that the main point of
our health care system is to make money.

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:49.500
The economy is hugely reliant on revenue from
health care, and that's not good when in my

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:54.810
view, the main goal of health care should
be to keep people and to make people healthy,

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:56.289
not to make money.

00:04:56.289 --> 00:05:00.050
This is a structural analysis that can be
done.

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:06.099
When you're trying to think about how an industry
functions, if we ask how do cable news channel,

00:05:06.099 --> 00:05:09.020
what is the main purpose of cable news channels?

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:11.620
Is it to inform people as much as possible?

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:13.590
No, it's to make money.

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:19.630
Once you understand the primary purpose of
an industry, you understand why they make

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:24.270
the editorial decisions that they make, et
cetera, so on and so forth.

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:28.199
If you look at the health care industry in
the United States, when you realize, well,

00:05:28.199 --> 00:05:32.600
it's primarily organized to be a profit center,
it's primarily organized to make money.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:36.259
It tells you a lot about why it fails in the
areas in which it fails.

00:05:36.259 --> 00:05:41.380
And what we are seeing during a medical pandemic,
during a viral pandemic, which is a medical

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:48.099
event, we are seeing one of the largest reductions
in GDP came from the health care industry

00:05:48.099 --> 00:05:53.410
because so much of that industry, of course,
you've got emergency rooms which are very

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:54.410
full.

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:56.550
You have ICU use, you have hospital beds.

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:59.770
But when you're looking at everything from,
you know, orthopedic, you need to have your

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:04.960
knee scope to arthroscopic surgery on your
knee, for example.

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:05.960
You're putting that off.

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:08.250
Orthopedic surgeries are very, very profitable.

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:09.250
You get the idea.

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:14.330
So when we look at those numbers, a large
portion of the decline in GDP in Q1 came from

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:17.330
within the healthcare industry.

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:20.449
So 4.8 percent decline in Q1.

00:06:20.449 --> 00:06:23.169
If we have a full quarter of locked.

00:06:23.169 --> 00:06:28.009
April, May, June, the numbers could be absolutely
devastating.

00:06:28.009 --> 00:06:31.500
Now, some states are starting to open probably
too soon.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:33.250
Remember, though.

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:34.650
Trump can't open the country.

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:37.430
Trump can't open states, governors can't even
really do it.

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:39.160
They can allow it.

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:44.569
But even if businesses start having employees
standing there waiting for you to come in.

00:06:44.569 --> 00:06:49.379
If people don't go in, the numbers are not
going to be dramatically better.

00:06:49.379 --> 00:06:55.789
My sense right now is May is going to be not
good, but it will probably be better than

00:06:55.789 --> 00:06:57.050
April.

00:06:57.050 --> 00:07:00.509
Hopefully June can be salvaged to some degree.

00:07:00.509 --> 00:07:04.629
And I mean that both economically, psychologically,
socially, culturally, everything.

00:07:04.629 --> 00:07:05.629
But we just don't know.

00:07:05.629 --> 00:07:06.889
We have to go where the data takes us.

00:07:06.889 --> 00:07:09.919
And right now, we are still having huge death
numbers.

00:07:09.919 --> 00:07:13.520
We I think yesterday we found another 24000
new cases.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:16.080
So we've been in a plateau that's lasted about
a full months.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:21.180
And I don't believe at the at the national
level, we are clearly on the other side of

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:22.180
it.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:23.280
States like New York are nationally.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:08:38.979
The numbers are still very, very high.

